>A zen character (one who is always blind) can read scrolls.  I
>understand that a shopkeeper can help you to know enough about a
>scroll to read it while blind.  

I think you're mistaken here. A quick wizmode test tells me that if
you sell an unidentified scroll to a shopkeeper, it remains
unidentified. And the only other ways I can think of to positively
identify a scroll (i.e., where the game knows it's id'd, so it will
let you "read" it while blind) are to read it, to cast a spell of
identify, or to read a scroll of identify and ID the scroll you want
to use. All of these would either break illiterate conduct or imply
that illiterate conduct was already broken (by previously having read
the ID scroll or spellbook). The only way I can see around it is to
start with a scroll or spellbook of ID on the first turn, which thus
would be preidentified for you.

>The only way this makes sense to me is
>if the shopkeeper (at least if you are blind) is willing to tell you
>the words on a scroll.  This does not and should not break conduct. 
>Spoken words and memorization are not literacy.

If the game did work this way, I would call it a bug. Shopkeepers
sometimes ID certain items, presumably by calling them by their
correct name in the shop. There is no warrant for imagining that a
shopkeeper actually teaches you the spell in the shop.
>
>A blind character with certain knowlege of a scroll can "read" it. 
>One argument would be that this does not break literacy.  The
>character can not see the words, so does not need to be able to read,
>that is, translate visual representations of words into mental ones. 
>If the character has not proven to be literate before this action,
>this action does not prove that the character is literate.

If I am correct in the above, then a blind person "reading" a scroll
would already have broken illiterate conduct, unless he started with a
scroll or spellbook of identify. Wizmode testing confirms that the
game takes "reading" a scroll while blind as a genuine read and breaks
illiterate conduct. I would call this legitimate, as it implies that
your character has read the spellbook/scroll at some point prior to
the game, and thus was not illiterate.
>
>Another argument might say that this is still proof of literacy. 
>Magic does not just require action, it requires specific thought
>patterns.  An illiterate character could perform the actions needed to
>evoke the magically effect, but still no effect would be produced
>without a certain amount of understanding of the symbols, i.e.
>literacy.  A blind character who is literate could still have this
>understanding.
>
>Personally, I find the first argument more compelling.  There isn't
>any intelligence requirement to read a scroll.  Reading a scroll in
>nethack really doesn't seem to require any sort of understanding at
>all.

No, but it requires literacy, which is why it breaks illiterate
conduct.
>
>We could also extend the argument further.  Why do you have to be
>blind?  Couldn't a sight-capable illiterate character do the same
>thing if he got a shopkeeper to read the scroll to him?  So perhaps
>even reading a scroll while sight-capable is ok, provided you were
>blind when the shopkeeper read you the scroll.
>
Illiterate conduct means that you don't read or write within the game.
Why do you want to figure out ways to read and still claim
"illiterate" conduct? I'm sure if you put your mind to it, you can
find ways to write without breaking the conduct, too. "Maybe my
character saw someone else scribble 'Elbereth' on something, and
copied the magical strokes, without recognizing that they were
letters. Maybe once I ID'd the 'enchant armor' scroll, I just
reproduced the funny-looking marks on it." Blah blah blah.

Why not just invent a "foo" conduct, which you can claim no matter
what it is you did in the game?

Sorry, but I don't buy all the within-the-game speculation. If you use
the "r"ead command, if you "E"ngrave anything but "X," if you "a"pply
a magic marker to a blank scroll or spellbook, then you've proved
you're literate. End of story.
